{
  "schema_version": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "id": "aws-three-tier-reference",
    "title": "AWS three-tier web application with EKS companion",
    "environment": "reference",
    "assumptions": [
      "Synthetic reference model for the T-001 brief and its EKS companion; nothing here describes deployed infrastructure or observed state.",
      "Region ap-southeast-1 and zones ap-southeast-1a and ap-southeast-1b are illustrative; the brief supplies no region or zone names.",
      "The region lists the account in scope_refs so the overview nests the region in the account once. It states that the account uses the region, not that an account owns a region.",
      "Each zone has a public, an app and a data subnet. The second data subnet holds no resource; it is declared because an RDS DB subnet group must span at least two zones.",
      "One RDS DB instance sits in the first zone's data subnet; no standby, replica or Multi-AZ deployment is modeled, and the engine, size and port are not supplied.",
      "Two application EC2 instances, one per app subnet, are an illustrative count; the brief gives no instance count, type or size. Add or rename instances without changing existing IDs.",
      "The ALB is one logical load balancer attached to both public subnets; it is not duplicated per zone.",
      "One security group per tier (ALB, application, database) is shown and listed on each tier's resources; the brief supplies no rules, so none are modeled.",
      "HTTPS is labeled on the Internet gateway to ALB hop (the client-facing listener). The brief names no protocol for the ALB-to-instance hop, so that hop is unlabeled.",
      "Internet clients are not modeled as an entity; the Internet gateway is the edge of the model.",
      "The application-to-database edge carries only that label from the brief.",
      "Egress: one zonal NAT gateway in the first public subnet serves both application instances and reaches the Internet gateway. Per-zone NAT redundancy was not supplied, so the second instance egresses across zones.",
      "S3 and CloudWatch are shown as regional services in the account. The brief states no link to either, so no relationship to them is drawn.",
      "The EKS cluster attaches to both app subnets, and its managed node group launches one Node in each; no subnets were added for Kubernetes.",
      "Kubernetes names, the namespace and a replica count of 2 are illustrative. The Deployment's two Pods run one per Node.",
      "The Ingress controller's implementation is not supplied; it is cluster-level with no namespace, and no load balancer is linked to it. It is not the web-tier ALB.",
      "The PVC is claimed by the Deployment, bound to a PV and uses a StorageClass; backing store, size and access mode are not supplied.",
      "The Secret is a reference marker with no value. NetworkPolicy rules and ServiceAccount permissions are not supplied.",
      "The overview shows the EKS cluster only; Kubernetes detail is in the eks-namespaces and eks-nodes views, so the overview lists those entities in view_exclusions."
    ],
    "description": "Reference model for scenario T-001: one account and region across two availability zones, with an Amazon EKS workload in the same account and VPC. Synthetic; it does not describe deployed infrastructure."
  },
